WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.273
Is there a shortcut for powers and roots
of complex numbers? You can write complex

00:04.273 --> 00:08.545
numbers in polar form. In this lesson we
raise complex numbers to powers and find

00:08.545 --> 00:12.818
roots using de Moivre's theorem. By the
end, Raise complex numbers to powers and

00:12.818 --> 00:17.091
find roots.

00:17.091 --> 00:21.615
De Moivre's theorem says that to raise a
polar complex number to a power, raise the

00:21.615 --> 00:26.139
modulus to that power and multiply the
angle by n.

00:26.139 --> 00:31.260
One plus i has modulus root two and angle
forty five degrees. Raising to the fourth

00:31.260 --> 00:36.381
power gives modulus four and angle one
hundred eighty degrees.

00:36.381 --> 00:39.647
Cosine of one hundred eighty degrees is
minus one and sine is zero, so the answer

00:39.647 --> 00:42.912
is minus four.

00:42.912 --> 00:47.969
The same idea finds roots: the cube roots
of one are equally spaced around the unit

00:47.969 --> 00:53.027
circle, at zero, one hundred twenty and
two hundred forty degrees.

00:53.027 --> 00:58.191
A common mistake is to multiply the
modulus by n. Pause and decide whether

00:58.191 --> 01:03.355
that is right. The modulus is raised to
the power n, not multiplied.

01:03.355 --> 01:08.209
Your turn. Evaluate one plus i to the
sixth power. Pause the video. Hint: Use

01:08.209 --> 01:13.064
modulus eight and angle two hundred
seventy degrees.

01:13.064 --> 01:18.036
The result is minus eight i. In a new
context, The cube roots of one are equally

01:18.036 --> 01:23.008
spaced around the unit circle. Rate your
confidence from one to five.

01:23.008 --> 01:27.852
De Moivre powers the modulus and
multiplies the angle. Roots are equally

01:27.852 --> 01:32.696
spaced around the circle. Next, we draw
loci in the complex plane.
